- How would you adapt this for counting 0-runs?
- What's the time complexity compared to a loop-based solution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft branchless 128-bit comparison, a common low-level operation in high-performance contexts.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless selection using masks
- 128-bit comparison via hi/lo parts
- Handling -1/0/1 outputs reliably

## Code Example

```c
int compare128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  int hi = (int)(a.hi > b.hi) - (int)(a.hi < b.hi);
  int lo = (int)(a.lo > b.lo) - (int)(a.lo < b.lo);
  int t  = (hi != 0);
  int m  = -t;
  return (hi & m) | (lo & ~m);
}
```

